  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=3,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =-{x}^{-2}(({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+(2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x))).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-(1-2{c}_{2}) \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}{x}.\n$$",
